---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';

const title = 'GraceLock - Privacy Policy';
const description = 'Privacy Policy for GraceLock - Prayer-Based Screen Time App';
---

<Base head={{ title, description }}>
	<Section>
		<article class="prose prose-gray dark:prose-invert max-w-4xl mx-auto">
			<h1>Privacy Policy for GraceLock</h1>
			<p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
				Last updated: November 25, 2026
			</p>

			<h2>Overview</h2>
			<p>
				GraceLock ("we", "our", or "the app") is committed to protecting your privacy. This Privacy Policy explains how we handle information when you use our prayer-based screen time management application.
			</p>

			<h2>Information We Collect</h2>
			<p>
				GraceLock is designed with privacy in mind. We collect minimal information necessary to provide our services:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>We don't require personal information like your name or email</li>
				<li>We don't access your contacts or photos</li>
				<li>We don't track your location</li>
				<li>We don't read or store the content of your blocked apps</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>App Blocking on iPhone and iPad</h2>
			<p>
				On iOS, GraceLock uses Apple's Screen Time APIs (FamilyControls, ManagedSettings, and DeviceActivity) to provide app blocking functionality. This means:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Your selection of which apps to block is stored locally on your device</li>
				<li>We cannot see what specific apps you have blocked - Apple's privacy design prevents this</li>
				<li>App blocking works entirely on-device through Apple's frameworks</li>
				<li>We receive only anonymous, aggregate information about app blocking events</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>App Blocking on Android</h2>
			<p>
				Android has no Screen Time API, so GraceLock uses Android's <strong>Accessibility service</strong> to block apps. You turn it on yourself in Android Settings after GraceLock explains what it does, and you can turn it off at any time.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>The service is used for one thing only: noticing which app has just opened, so GraceLock can show the prayer screen when it is one of the apps you chose to block</li>
				<li>It does not read what is on your screen, what you type, or what you do inside your apps</li>
				<li>Nothing the service sees is stored beyond the moment, sent to our servers, or shared with anyone</li>
				<li>Your list of blocked apps is stored only on your device</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				If you can't use the Accessibility service, GraceLock can instead use <strong>Usage Access</strong> and <strong>Display over other apps</strong> for the same purpose: checking on your device which app is in the foreground and showing the prayer screen over a blocked app. This information also stays on your device. Analytics events about blocking (for example that the prayer screen was shown) do not include which app you blocked.
			</p>

			<h2>Prayer and Usage Data</h2>
			<p>
				To provide prayer tracking features, we store the following locally on your device:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Prayer completion records and streaks</li>
				<li>Prayer journal entries (premium feature)</li>
				<li>App usage statistics</li>
				<li>Your preferences and settings</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				This data is stored locally and is not transmitted to our servers unless you explicitly use a sync feature.
			</p>

			<h2>Analytics and Crash Reporting</h2>
			<p>
				To improve the app experience, we use the following services:
			</p>

			<h3>Firebase Analytics</h3>
			<p>
				We use Firebase Analytics to understand general app usage patterns. This includes:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Feature usage statistics (anonymized)</li>
				<li>App launch frequency</li>
				<li>Onboarding completion rates</li>
				<li>Device type and OS version</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				This data is anonymized and aggregated. We cannot identify individual users from this information.
			</p>

			<h3>Firebase Crashlytics</h3>
			<p>
				We use Firebase Crashlytics to identify and fix app crashes. When the app crashes, it may send:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Device model and OS version</li>
				<li>App version</li>
				<li>Crash stack traces</li>
				<li>Anonymous installation UUID</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Advertising Measurement (Meta)</h2>
			<p>
				We advertise GraceLock on Facebook and Instagram. To learn which ads lead to people installing and using the app, GraceLock includes the Meta (Facebook) SDK. It is used only to measure our own ads. It does not show ads in the app, and it does not use Facebook Login.
			</p>
			<p>
				On iOS, the app asks on first launch whether GraceLock may track you, and nothing is sent to Meta before you answer. After that, the app sends Meta:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>App installs and app launches</li>
				<li>A few milestone events: finishing setup, completing your first prayer to unlock an app, and seeing an offer to upgrade</li>
				<li>Subscriptions, free trials and other purchases made through the App Store or Google Play</li>
				<li>Device information such as model, OS version and app version</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>If you allow tracking</strong>, these events also include your device's advertising identifier (IDFA), which Meta may link to your Facebook or Instagram account to measure and improve ads. <strong>If you ask the app not to track</strong>, no advertising identifier is sent, and Meta receives only the events above without it, for aggregated ad measurement through Apple's SKAdNetwork.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>On Android</strong>, the same events are sent together with your device's advertising ID, which Meta may link to your Facebook or Instagram account to measure ads. You can reset or delete the advertising ID in Android Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Ads (or Google &rarr; Ads), after which it is no longer sent.
			</p>
			<p>
				We never send your prayers, moods, journal entries, streaks, blocked apps or any Screen Time or Accessibility data to Meta. You can change your choice at any time in iOS Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr; Tracking. Meta's use of this data is covered by the <a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meta Privacy Policy</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>Prayer Library</h2>
			<p>
				GraceLock syncs prayer content from Firebase Firestore. This is a one-way sync where we deliver prayer content to your device. We do not upload your personal prayers or journal entries to our servers.
			</p>

			<h2>In-App Purchases</h2>
			<p>
				GraceLock offers premium features through subscriptions. Purchase processing is handled by:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Apple App Store</strong> on iPhone and iPad, and <strong>Google Play</strong> on Android</li>
				<li><strong>RevenueCat</strong> for subscription management</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				We don't store payment information. All transactions are processed securely by Apple or Google and RevenueCat.
			</p>

			<h2>Third-Party Services</h2>
			<p>
				GraceLock uses the following third-party services:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Firebase</a> - Analytics, crash reporting, and prayer content delivery</li>
				<li><a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RevenueCat</a> - Subscription management</li>
				<li><a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meta</a> - Advertising measurement</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Children's Privacy</h2>
			<p>
				GraceLock may be used by families with teenagers under parental guidance. We don't knowingly collect personal information from children under 13. The app doesn't require any personal information to function.
			</p>

			<h2>Your Rights</h2>
			<p>
				You have control over your data:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Delete the app at any time to remove all local data</li>
				<li>Disable analytics collection in your device settings</li>
				<li>Clear your prayer journal and history within the app</li>
				<li>Revoke Screen Time permissions at any time in iOS Settings, or turn off GraceLock in Android Settings &rarr; Accessibility</li>
				<li>Turn off tracking for GraceLock in iOS Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr; Tracking, or reset or delete your advertising ID on Android</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>Deleting Your Data</h3>
			<p>
				Deleting the app removes everything GraceLock stores on your device. To stop sending advertising data, turn off tracking as described above. If you have a Facebook or Instagram account, you can view and clear activity that apps have shared with Meta in Accounts Center under "Your activity off Meta technologies".
			</p>

			<h2>Data Security</h2>
			<p>
				We take appropriate measures to protect your information:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>All data transmission uses HTTPS encryption</li>
				<li>Local data is protected by your device's security</li>
				<li>We follow Apple's privacy guidelines for Screen Time APIs and Google Play's policies for the Accessibility API</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
			<p>
				We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. We will notify you of any changes by updating the "Last updated" date at the top of this policy.
			</p>

			<h2>Contact</h2>
			<p>
				If you have questions about this Privacy Policy or how GraceLock handles data, email <a href="mailto:gracelock@tskulbru.dev">gracelock@tskulbru.dev</a>.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg">
				<p class="text-sm text-center text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
					<a href="/" class="hover:text-orange-600 transition-colors">&larr; Back to tskulbru.dev</a> |
					<a href="/apps/gracelock-support" class="hover:text-orange-600 transition-colors">Support</a> |
					<a href="/apps/gracelock-terms" class="hover:text-orange-600 transition-colors">Terms of Use</a>
				</p>
			</div>
		</article>
	</Section>
</Base>
